    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d663">

                <pb facs="0771" n="669" sameAs="frus:frus1919v01/pg669"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.119/273</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Reinsch</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Acting Secretary of
                    State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1919-05-10"><hi rendition="#italic">May 10,
                            1919.</hi></date><lb/>[<hi rendition="#italic">Received June
                        7.</hi>]</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 2725</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: In continuation of my despatch No. 2663 of
                    April 4th<anchor n="94" xml:id="d663-f1"/> I have the honor to enclose copies of
                    the following documents: (1) an answer from the Japanese Minister dated April
                        13th<anchor n="94" xml:id="d663-f2"/> to my note of April 3rd,<anchor n="94"
                        xml:id="d663-f3"/> and (2) joint note sent to the Foreign Office on May 5th
                    relating to the restriction of import of arms and ammunition into China.</p>
                <p>In this connection I have the honor to report that at a meeting of the Diplomatic
                    Corps on May 3rd when the above action was definitely decided upon, I made the
                    statement that it was my understanding that the restrictions contemplated
                    included all the Leased Territories in China such as, among others, Tsingtau and
                    Dairen. This understanding was specifically stated to be correct by all the
                    Ministers present, including the British, French and Japanese.</p>
                <p>At a meeting of the Diplomatic Corps on April 26th in which it was decided upon
                    principle to take the action reported on, I stated that the American Government
                    would not after this issue export licenses for arms and ammunition with
                    destination to China; and that therefore no arms or ammunition not shipped
                    before that date could be imported into China. I repeated this statement on May
                    3rd.</p>
                <p>The reception which will be given to the action of the Powers in this matter by
                    the different parties in China is not yet apparent. The interruption of all
                    telegraphic communication keeps matters at a standstill.</p>
                <p>I have [etc.]</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Paul S. Reinsch</hi></signed></closer>



                <xi:include href="d663-a1.xml" xpointer="d663-a1"/>
